#
# Hand-authored (Batch A scenario-authoring task, 2026-08-20) -- NOT a
# generator stub. emit_oracles() never overwrites this file once it exists
# (specs/SCHEMA.md §8.2 rule 7).
#
# Scenario:   s3-bucket-hardening-decomposition (specs/s3-bucket-hardening-decomposition.yaml)
# Intent doc: oracles/s3-bucket-hardening-decomposition/intent.md
# Graded against the awscdk arm's synthesized CloudFormation template
# (cdk.out/ScenarioStack.template.json) -- specs/SCHEMA.md §4.2/§8. A
# generated tests/static_tiers.sh runs
#   cfn-guard validate --data cdk.out/ScenarioStack.template.json --rules policy.guard
# and fails tier-1 iff any rule below FAILs.
#
# Encodes two tier-1 structural_assert families
# (specs/s3-bucket-hardening-decomposition.yaml), both direct/static checks
# since CFN's synthesized template is always fully static (no "plan"
# concept, unlike the TF-shaped arms -- SCHEMA.md §4.2.1):
#
#   1. tls-deny-covers-objects-too-cfn -- the Deny-on-non-TLS statement's
#      Resource array has at least 2 elements. VERIFIED DIRECTLY at
#      authoring time against a real `cdk synth` output (aws-cdk-lib
#      2.263.0, offline): `s3.Bucket({ enforceSSL: true, ... })`
#      synthesizes a Resource array of exactly 2 elements -- an
#      `Fn::GetAtt` node for the bucket ARN and an `Fn::Join` node for the
#      object-ARN pattern (`aws-s3/lib/bucket.ts:2689-2704`,
#      `arnForObjects('*')`). The plausible-wrong fixture (Resource naming
#      only the bucket ARN) has exactly 1.
#
#      SCOPING FIX (this authoring pass, 2026-08-21, verifier-found major):
#      the cardinality check below is scoped to `%tls_deny_statements` --
#      Deny statements that ARE the TLS-deny (matched by their own
#      `Condition.Bool.'aws:SecureTransport' == "false"`), never to every
#      Deny statement in the policy. The rule used to run `count(Resource.*)
#      >= 2` against `%deny_statements` (every Deny, unscoped) -- a fully
#      correct template that adds any SECOND, legitimately bucket-scoped
#      Deny statement (e.g. `enforceSSL: true` plus `minimumTLSVersion:
#      "1.2"`, which aws-cdk-lib's own `s3.Bucket` L2 renders as a SEPARATE
#      Deny conditioned on `NumericLessThan.'s3:TlsVersion'`, with no Bool
#      condition at all -- or a hand-added Deny denying
#      `s3:PutBucketPolicy`/`s3:PutBucketAcl` scoped to the bucket ARN
#      alone, by design a 1-element Resource array) failed this rule even
#      though the TLS-deny statement ITSELF covered both ARNs. VERIFIED
#      DIRECTLY (this authoring pass): a template with the reference
#      solution's own TLS-deny (2-element Resource) plus a second,
#      legitimately 1-element-Resource Deny (`s3:PutBucketPolicy` /
#      `s3:PutBucketAcl` scoped to the bucket ARN) -- `cfn-guard validate`
#      against the OLD unscoped rule: `Status = FAIL`,
#      `tls_deny_covers_bucket_and_objects FAIL`, exit 19. Same template
#      against the scoped rule below: `Status = PASS`, exit 0. The
#      plausible-wrong fixture (single Deny, single-element Resource, IS
#      itself the TLS-deny) still fails the scoped rule identically to
#      before (verified: `Status = FAIL`, exit 19) -- the fix narrows what
#      the cardinality check applies to, not what it requires.
#
#   2. sse-kms-references-created-key-cfn -- BucketEncryption's
#      KMSMasterKeyID is an `Fn::GetAtt` intrinsic (mirrors
#      oracles/cfn-guard/s3-lambda-log-retention/policy.guard's own
#      SourceArn.'Fn::GetAtt' pattern exactly, same rationale: CDK's own
#      `s3.Bucket` L2 always renders a same-configuration KMS key's ARN as
#      `Fn::GetAtt`; a hardcoded/imported key ARN literal is a plain string
#      and can never have that key). VERIFIED DIRECTLY: a fixture using
#      `kms.Key.fromKeyArn(...)` (the kms-key-not-referenced catch's own
#      mechanism) synthesizes `KMSMasterKeyID` as a bare string with NO
#      `Fn::GetAtt` key at all, and ZERO `AWS::KMS::Key` resources in the
#      template.
#
# Residual, documented gap (mirrors s3-lambda-log-retention's own header
# comment for the identical class of limitation, cfn-guard 3.2.0 has no
# cross-collection identity-join builtin): rule 2 cannot verify the
# `Fn::GetAtt` target's logical ID actually names an AWS::KMS::Key resource
# (as opposed to some other GetAtt-addressable resource) -- an adversarial
# template that GetAtt's an unrelated resource's Arn would still pass. Not
# defended against here, consistent with this codebase's existing
# convention of treating such adversarial-construction gaps as an accepted
# v1 idealization when the realistic mistake (an imported/hardcoded key
# ARN literal) is what's covered.
#
# every-subresource-targets-this-bucket-tf has NO cfn-guard counterpart:
# CFN's AWS::S3::Bucket carries VersioningConfiguration / BucketEncryption /
# PublicAccessBlockConfiguration as INLINE properties on the one bucket
# resource -- there is no separate-subresource concept to misattach on this
# arm at all (the spec's own subresource-targets-wrong-bucket catch is
# `applies_to: [hcl_raw, terraconstructs]` for exactly this reason).

let s3_buckets = Resources.*[ Type == "AWS::S3::Bucket" ]
let bucket_policies = Resources.*[ Type == "AWS::S3::BucketPolicy" ]
let deny_statements = Resources.*[
	Type == "AWS::S3::BucketPolicy"
].Properties.PolicyDocument.Statement[ Effect == "Deny" ]
# The TLS-deny statement specifically -- scoped by its OWN condition, not
# just "any Deny" (see this file's header comment, "SCOPING FIX"). A
# second, legitimately bucket-ARN-only Deny statement (a different
# concern entirely) must never be subject to the "covers bucket AND
# objects" cardinality check below, since that check's whole rationale
# (the TLS-deny statement must reach both the bucket ARN and the object
# ARNs) simply does not apply to it.
let tls_deny_statements = Resources.*[
	Type == "AWS::S3::BucketPolicy"
].Properties.PolicyDocument.Statement[
	Effect == "Deny"
	Condition.Bool.'aws:SecureTransport' == "false"
]

rule bucket_policy_exists_when_bucket_present when %s3_buckets !empty {
	%bucket_policies !empty
	<<
		Violation: an AWS::S3::Bucket exists but no AWS::S3::BucketPolicy resource was found in the template -- the TLS-deny requirement cannot be satisfied without one
	>>
}

# tls-deny-covers-objects-too-cfn
#
# AGGREGATE-ACROSS-STATEMENTS FIX (verifier-found major, 2026-08-22 third
# verification pass): the cardinality check used to run `count(Resource.*)
# >= 2` INSIDE the `%tls_deny_statements { ... }` block, which applies the
# check to EACH matched statement INDIVIDUALLY. A logically identical,
# equally correct template that expresses the TLS deny as TWO SEPARATE
# statements -- one Resource=[bucket ARN], one Resource=[object-ARN
# pattern], both conditioned on aws:SecureTransport=="false" -- has every
# individual statement's own Resource count at 1, so the per-statement form
# FAILED it even though the two statements TOGETHER cover both ARNs.
# VERIFIED DIRECTLY (cfn-guard 3.2.0): a hand-built two-statement template
# of exactly this shape -- old rule: `Status = FAIL`,
# `tls_deny_covers_bucket_and_objects FAIL`, `ComparisonError Value=1
# ComparedWith=2`, exit 19; new rule below (aggregated `count`): `Status =
# PASS`, exit 0. This is precisely the rego oracle's own semantics
# (oracles/rego/.../policy.rego's `bucket_ref_count` counts references
# across the WHOLE policy expression, not per-statement), so this fix also
# restores arm parity between the CFN and TF-shaped checks for this catch.
# Re-verified unchanged after this fix: the plausible-wrong single-statement
# fixture (Resource=[bucket ARN] only) still FAILS (exit 19); a template
# with no TLS-deny statement at all still FAILS closed (exit 19, no
# non-compliant resource crash from evaluating `.Resource.*` over an empty
# collection); the existing fix-(b) shape (correct 2-element TLS deny plus
# an unrelated, legitimately 1-element `s3:PutBucketAcl` Deny) still PASSES
# (exit 0) -- aggregation only changes the split-TLS-statement outcome.
rule tls_deny_covers_bucket_and_objects when %deny_statements !empty {
	%tls_deny_statements !empty
	<<
		Violation: no Deny statement conditioned on Bool['aws:SecureTransport'] == "false" was found -- "any request not made over TLS must be rejected" is not satisfied
	>>
	let tls_deny_resource_count = count(%tls_deny_statements.Resource.*)
	%tls_deny_resource_count >= 2
	<<
		Violation: the TLS-deny statement(s)' combined Resource coverage has fewer than 2 elements -- it must cover both the bucket ARN itself and every object inside it (an object-ARN pattern), whether expressed as one Deny statement or split across several, or requests to objects over plain HTTP remain allowed
	>>
}

# sse-kms-references-created-key-cfn
rule sse_kms_key_exists_when_bucket_present when %s3_buckets !empty {
	%s3_buckets {
		when Properties.BucketEncryption.ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration[0].ServerSideEncryptionByDefault.SSEAlgorithm == "aws:kms" {
			Properties.BucketEncryption.ServerSideEncryptionConfiguration[0].ServerSideEncryptionByDefault.KMSMasterKeyID.'Fn::GetAtt' EXISTS
			<<
				Violation: SSEAlgorithm is aws:kms but KMSMasterKeyID is not an Fn::GetAtt reference to a KMS key resource declared in this template (an imported/hardcoded key ARN literal is not verifiably "a key we control" from a static artifact)
			>>
		}
	}
}
